WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.010
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:11.610
Today we are going to create beautiful virtual
clothes and marvel at how quickly we can simulate

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:12.889
them.

00:00:12.889 --> 00:00:18.830
And the kicker is that these simulations are
able to create details that are so tiny, they

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:22.399
are smaller than a millimeter.

00:00:22.399 --> 00:00:27.289
When I first saw this title of this paper,
I had two thoughts.

00:00:27.289 --> 00:00:30.439
First I asked, submillimeter level?

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:32.150
Is this really necessary?

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:38.050
Well, here is a dress rendered at the level
of millimeters.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:42.730
And here is what it would look like with this
new simulation technique.

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:47.290
Hm, so many crisp details suddenly appeared.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:51.760
Okay, you got me, I am now converted.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:58.570
So let’s proceed to the second issue - here
I also said I will believe this kind of quality

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:01.040
in a simulation when I see it.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.200
So, how does this work?

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:10.310
Well, we can give it a piece of coarse input
geometry, and this new technique synthesizes

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:13.859
and simulates additional wrinkles on it.

00:01:13.859 --> 00:01:19.710
For instance, here, we can add these vertical
shirring patterns to it.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:25.749
And not only that, but we can still manage
collision detection, so it can interact with

00:01:25.749 --> 00:01:31.850
other objects and suddenly, the whole piece
looks beautifully lifelike.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:37.740
And as you see here, the direction of these
patterns can be chosen by us, and, it gets

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:42.149
better, because it can handle other kinds
of patterns too.

00:01:42.149 --> 00:01:48.049
I am a light transport researcher by trade,
and I am very pleased by how beautifully these

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:51.279
specular highlights are playing with the light.

00:01:51.279 --> 00:01:52.529
So good.

00:01:52.529 --> 00:01:55.749
Now, let’s see it in practice.

00:01:55.749 --> 00:02:02.159
On the left, you see the coarse geometry input,
and on the right, you see the magical new

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:05.549
clothes this new method can create from them.

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:08.580
And, yes, this is it!

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:09.580
Finally!

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:15.300
I always wondered why virtual characters with
simulated clothes always looked a little flat

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.480
for many years now.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.250
These are the details that were missing.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.050
Just look at the difference this makes!

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:24.170
Loving it.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:27.810
But, how does this stack up agains previous
methods?

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:32.200
Well, here is a previous technique from last
year.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.740
And here is the new one.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.190
Okay.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.880
How do we know which one is really better?

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:45.230
Well, we do it in terms of mathematics, and,
when looking at the relative errors from a

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:49.040
reference simulation, the new one is more
accurate.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:50.060
Great.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:54.660
But, hold on to your papers, because here
comes the best part!

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:55.660
Whoa!

00:02:55.660 --> 00:03:00.470
It is not only more accurate, but it is blistering
fast.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:04.500
Easily eight times faster than this previous
method.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:09.410
So, where does this put us in terms of total
execution time?

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:15.380
At approximately one second per frame, often
even less.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:16.380
What?

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:23.730
One second per image for a crisp, submillimeter
level cloth simulation that is also quite

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:24.940
accurate?

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:26.150
Wow.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:30.820
The pace of progress in computer graphics
research is absolutely amazing.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:36.720
And just imagine what we will be able to do
just two more papers down the line.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.240
This might run in real time easily.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.180
Now, drawbacks.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:47.740
Well, not really a drawback, but there are
cases where the submillimeter simulation results

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:53.330
materialize in a bit more crisp creases…but
not much more.

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:56.680
I really had to go to hunt for differences
in this one.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:03.140
If you have spotted stark differences between
the two here, please let me know in the comments.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:10.030
And here comes one more amazing thing: this
paper was written by Huamin Wang and…that’s

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:11.030
it.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:17.190
A single author paper that has been accepted
to the SIGGRAPH conference, which is perhaps

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:20.040
the most prestigious conference in computer
graphics.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.970
That is a rare sight indeed.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:50.210
You see, sometimes even a single researcher

00:04:50.210 --> 00:05:07.710
can 

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:19.560
make all the difference.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:20.560
Huge congratulations!

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.380
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

